(The gen loop: generate, compile, run, classify, record.

 The invariant being tested: a vox-emitted binary must never die by
 signal and never hang. A nonzero exit code is normal and is not a
 finding.)

see "./sandbox.vox".

a number called stat_total is 0.
a number called stat_compiled is 0.
a number called stat_findings is 0.

(Pure compiler-exit-code classifier, extracted from 'fuzz gen once' so the
 exit-code -> category mapping can be unit-tested without compiling or
 running anything. Returns the category the codegen loop acts on:
   code < 0      -> "hang"       (compiler exceeded its budget)
   code > 128    -> "ice"        (compiler died by signal; 128+N is signal N)
   code == 101   -> "ice"        (rust panic)
   code == 0     -> "ok"         (compiled, proceed to run)
   otherwise     -> "check-asm"  (1..128: a benign failure OR an asm-reject,
                                  decided by the toolchain grep in 'fuzz gen
                                  once', which needs the compiler's stderr and
                                  so cannot be pure)
 The 128/129 boundary is the one this exists to pin: 128 is a legitimate
 exit code, 129 is a signal death. A naive `>= 128` would misclassify a real
 exit-128 as a crash.)
To 'classify compile exit' with a number called code.
    If code is less than 0 then, Return a text, "hang".
    If code is greater than 128 then, Return a text, "ice".
    If code is equal to 101 then, Return a text, "ice".
    If code is equal to 0 then, Return a text, "ok".
    Return a text, "check-asm".

(The ledger's assertion channel. docs/ledger/PROCEDURE.md §6 reserves
 exit 95 for a leaf assertion: the generator knows what it emitted, so
 it emits the check too, and a generated program that finds a documented
 result wrong prints one line

     ASSERT BUF-05: expected 4097 got 4096

 and exits 95. 91-94 stay the argv assertions; nothing generated exits
 90-99 for any other reason ('gen program's exit-code draw skips the
 whole band).

 The exit code alone only says "some ledger assertion failed". The line
 says WHICH claim and by how much, which is the difference between a
 finding a human can triage and one they have to re-run. So the line is
 lifted back out of the run's captured stdout - already captured for the
 differential oracle, so nothing new has to be retained - and carried as
 the finding's detail.

 LAST match, not first: a program asserts many times before one fails,
 and every earlier assertion that HELD prints nothing. Only the failing
 one is followed by the exit, so the last ASSERT line is the one that
 killed the program.

 The scan works on BYTES, not on text, and that is not fussiness. A
 generated program's stdout can contain NUL bytes - the format-string
 leaves open real file handles, and a captured stdout with a sparse hole
 in it reads back as a run of NULs (gen_files.vox records how that was
 found). A text stops at the first NUL, so routing the capture through
 'read whole file' dropped everything after it, ASSERT line included:
 proved by breaking a BUF-05 assertion on purpose, where 3 of 7 findings
 came back saying no ASSERT line was on stdout while the line was
 plainly the last thing the program printed. Reading into a dynamic
 buffer keeps every byte - one Read takes a 5,050-byte file with 5,000
 NULs in the middle, hand-verified - so the scan sees the whole
 capture.

 Reading every byte is not enough on its own, and the D1 addendum (the /dev/stdout reopen, 2026-08-20) is
 what the difference costs. A program that opens /dev/stdout while its
 stdout IS the capture file reopens that file at offset 0 and writes
 there, while the descriptor the ordinary Prints go through keeps its
 own offset - so the capture comes back as a short first line, a hole of
 NULs, and then the rest, ASSERT line included. Retained repro:
 vox-notes/env-a-seed-40252, seed 40252, whose capture is 205 bytes -
 `fmt4 hello4 0\n`, 158 NULs, then
 `ASSERT ENV-06: expected 9 got -1\n`.

 The hole holds no newline, so the last newline before the ASSERT line
 is the one at byte 14, and a scan that starts lines only after a
 NEWLINE was still testing the marker at byte 15 - a NUL - when it
 reached the end. The finding said "no ASSERT line was found on stdout"
 about a capture whose last line is the ASSERT. Swapping one NUL of that
 capture for a newline made the same scan find the line, which is the
 whole mechanism. So a NUL ends a line here too: it is the hole, not
 something a program printed, and the line after it starts where it
 ends.)

a text called 'the missing assert line' is "ledger assertion failed (exit 95) but no ASSERT line was found on stdout".

(Does the ASSERT marker sit at exactly this position in the captured
 output? Split out so the line scan below stays one loop deep - Vox
 closes clauses by period, and a second nested While inside the scan
 would put four levels of termination on one statement.)

To 'the assert marker starts at' with a buffer called 'the captured bytes' and a number called start.
    a buffer called 'the assert marker' is "ASSERT ".
    a number called 'the marker length' is 'the assert marker's size.
    a number called 'the last byte' is 'the captured bytes's size.
    a number called 'the offset' is 0.
    a boolean called 'every marker byte matched' is true.
    While 'the offset' is less than 'the marker length',
        a number called 'the captured position' is start add 'the offset',
        (0 for a position past the end - no byte can equal it, so a
         truncated final line fails the match instead of reading out of
         bounds and raising the error flag.)
        a number called 'the captured byte' is 0,
        If 'the captured position' is less than or equal to 'the last byte' then,
            Set 'the captured byte' to byte 'the captured position' of 'the captured bytes'.
        a number called 'the marker position' is 'the offset' add 1,
        a number called 'the marker byte' is byte 'the marker position' of 'the assert marker',
        If 'the captured byte' is not 'the marker byte' then,
            Set 'every marker byte matched' to false.
        Set 'the offset' to 'the offset' add 1.
    Return a boolean, 'every marker byte matched'.

(The last `ASSERT ...` line of a captured stdout, or a stand-in saying
 there was none.

 The scan runs one past the last byte and treats that position as a
 newline, so a final line with no trailing newline is still considered -
 a program killed mid-Print would otherwise have its ASSERT line
 silently dropped, which is the one case where the detail matters most.)

To 'the assert line in bytes' with a buffer called 'the captured bytes'.
    a number called 'the byte count' is 'the captured bytes's size.
    a number called 'the scan limit' is 'the byte count' add 1.
    a number called 'the line start' is 1.
    a number called 'the found start' is 0.
    a number called 'the found end' is 0.
    a number called 'the position' is 1.
    While 'the position' is less than or equal to 'the scan limit',
        a number called 'the byte here' is 10,
        If 'the position' is less than or equal to 'the byte count' then,
            Set 'the byte here' to byte 'the position' of 'the captured bytes'.
        (A NUL ends a line here exactly as a newline does. It is not part
         of any line a program printed - it is the hole - and the line
         the ASSERT is on starts after it, not at the last newline before
         it. See the note above for the capture this was proven on.)
        a boolean called 'this ends the line' is false,
        If 'the byte here' is 10 then,
            Set 'this ends the line' to true.
        If 'the byte here' is 0 then,
            Set 'this ends the line' to true.
        If 'this ends the line' then,
            a boolean called 'this line is an assertion' is 'the assert marker starts at' of 'the captured bytes' and 'the line start',
            If 'this line is an assertion' then,
                Set 'the found start' to 'the line start',
                Set 'the found end' to 'the position' minus 1.
            Set 'the line start' to 'the position' add 1.
        Set 'the position' to 'the position' add 1.
    If 'the found start' is 0 then,
        Return a text, 'the missing assert line'.
    (A findings detail is one line in classification.txt; a runaway
     ASSERT line would be a runaway detail, so the copy stops at the
     destination's capacity rather than silently half-failing on a
     Set byte past it.)
    a buffer called 'the assertion bytes' is 1024 bytes in size.
    a number called 'the copy limit' is 'the found start' add 1023.
    If 'the found end' is greater than 'the copy limit' then,
        Set 'the found end' to 'the copy limit'.
    a number called 'the copy position' is 'the found start'.
    a number called 'the write position' is 1.
    While 'the copy position' is less than or equal to 'the found end',
        a number called 'the byte copied' is byte 'the copy position' of 'the captured bytes',
        Set byte 'the write position' of 'the assertion bytes' to 'the byte copied',
        Set 'the write position' to 'the write position' add 1,
        Set 'the copy position' to 'the copy position' add 1.
    a text called 'the assertion line' is 'the assertion bytes' as text.
    Return a text, 'the assertion line'.

(The same scan over a capture still on disk, which is how the gen loop
 reaches it. Read into a DYNAMIC buffer rather than through 'read whole
 file': see the note above for the NUL bytes that makes the difference.)

To 'the assert line in file' with a text called 'the capture path'.
    (Consume whatever the rest of the loop left pending first. Vox's
     error flag stays set until an On error takes it, so without this the
     handlers below could be answering for a shell call three steps back
     and report no ASSERT line on a capture that has one. Same trap the
     BUF-03 leaf hit from the other side, in gen_buffers.vox.)
    a boolean called 'an error was already pending' is false.
    On error Set 'an error was already pending' to true.
    open a file for reading called 'the capture' at 'the capture path'.
    On error Return a text, 'the missing assert line'.
    a buffer called 'the captured bytes'.
    Read from 'the capture' into 'the captured bytes'.
    On error Return a text, 'the missing assert line'.
    Close 'the capture'.
    a text called 'the assertion found' is 'the assert line in bytes' of 'the captured bytes'.
    Return a text, 'the assertion found'.

(Text in, for the unit tests: same scan, over bytes the caller spelled
 out rather than bytes a program printed. Safe here because a Vox text
 literal cannot contain a NUL in the first place, which is exactly why
 it is not what the gen loop uses.)

To 'the assert line in' with a text called 'the captured output'.
    a buffer called 'the given bytes' is "{'the captured output'}".
    a text called 'the assertion found' is 'the assert line in bytes' of 'the given bytes'.
    Return a text, 'the assertion found'.

(--keep: copy each generated program somewhere durable BEFORE the sweep.
 Without this a passing program is unreadable by design — the scratch dir
 that holds it is removed the moment the run ends, which is correct for
 hygiene and useless for a human who wants to READ what the generator
 produces. Findings already survive (finding save copies the source
 out); this does the same for the ordinary case.

 Empty means off, so the default behaviour is unchanged and no campaign
 silently starts filling a directory.)
a text called keep_dir is "".

To 'keep source' with a text called 'the source path' and a number called seed.
    If keep_dir is empty then,
        Return a number, 0.
    a number called 'the make' is 'make directories' of keep_dir.
    a text called 'the destination' is "{keep_dir}/seed_{seed}.vox".
    a text called 'the copy command' is "cp {'shell quote' of 'the source path'} {'shell quote' of 'the destination'}".
    a number called 'the copy exit code' is 'run shell' of 'the copy command' and 5000.
    Return a number, 'the copy exit code'.

To 'fuzz gen once' with a number called seed and a number called budget and a number called 'the deadline in milliseconds'.
    Set stat_total to stat_total add 1.
    'gen program' of seed and budget.
    (Mirror the layout mode into the findings store, so every finding
     records which whitespace the program that ran was written in.)
    Set findings_layout to gen_layout.
    (D1: every artefact of this seed's compile/run lives under its own
     pid+seed scratch directory from sandbox.vox, so a second vox-fuzz
     process running the same seed range concurrently cannot overwrite
     this source, binary, or cerr. sandbox.vox's path is collision-free
     across OS pids AND across seeds within one run - see its header for
     why both halves of pid+seed matter. Vox has no try/finally, so the
     sweep is repeated before every Return: finding save reads the source
     to copy program.vox into the findings dir BEFORE the sweep, so the
     order finding-save -> sweep -> return keeps the copy intact while
     still leaving no scratch behind on any path.)
    a text called 'the scratch path' is 'scratch dir path' of seed.
    'scratch dir create' of 'the scratch path'.
    a text called 'the source path' is "{'the scratch path'}/vf_gen.vox".
    (Plan 327 Part B: the bytes this program will find on stdin, written
     into its own per-run scratch directory. Both of the oracle's runs
     are given this same path — if they were given different input, the
     difference between the runs would BE the input and every program
     would be a false nondeterminism finding.)
    a text called 'the input path' is "{'the scratch path'}/vf_input".
    'gen write input' of 'the input path'.
    (The program is told its own scratch directory on the command line,
     which is what lets it do real file I/O without an allowlist: it can
     only write where it was told, and this directory is pid-and-seed
     keyed and swept when the run ends. Set into the argv the generator
     built, so BOTH of the oracle's runs get the same value — a
     different scratch path per run would make the program's own output
     differ and every program a false nondeterminism finding.)
    (Only when this program declared a scratch flag - it does exactly
     when it chose file I/O, and a program that did not is invoked with
     no scratch argument at all. Verified against 0.4.9 that an
     undeclared flag on argv is neither a compile nor a runtime error,
     so passing one anyway would have been harmless; there is simply no
     reason to hand a program an argument it cannot read.)
    (FIRST, not appended last. 'gen build argv' shape 8 (gen_misc.vox)
     deliberately ends the command line with a LONE alias, to exercise
     the flag whose value is missing because argv ran out - so anything
     put after it becomes that flag's value. Appending the scratch pair
     there handed the scratch ALIAS to the hostile flag and left the
     scratch flag empty: the program then built its file paths from ""
     and wrote to /buffer4, which it cannot, so its own BUF-07 assertion
     failed on a truncated read that never happened and the run came
     back as a wrong-value finding. Found at seed 5018 of the acceptance
     campaign, reproduced by hand as `./prog -y -n <dir>` against the
     same binary. The scratch pair is the harness's plumbing, not part
     of the shape under test, so it goes in front where no drawn shape
     can reach it - and the hostile shapes still end the line exactly as
     they intend.)
    (Capture the drawn flags BEFORE the scratch pair is prepended, so
     repro.sh can replay them without the dead pid+seed scratch path the
     harness sweeps. findings_argv holds the clean flags; the scratch
     alias goes separately as findings_scratch_flag, and repro.sh makes
     its own fresh directory under it. Set here, before the prepend
     below rebinds gen_argv to a new list.)
    Set findings_argv to gen_argv.
    Set findings_scratch_flag to gen_scratch_argv.
    If gen_scratch_argv is not "" then,
        a list called 'the argv with the scratch directory' is [gen_scratch_argv, 'the scratch path'],
        For each 'the drawn argument' in gen_argv, append 'the drawn argument' to 'the argv with the scratch directory'.
        Set gen_argv to 'the argv with the scratch directory'.
    'gen write' of 'the source path'.
    a number called 'the kept' is 'keep source' of 'the source path' and seed.
    a text called 'the binary path' is "{'the scratch path'}/vf_gen_bin".
    a text called 'the cerr path' is "{'the scratch path'}/vf_cerr".
    a number called 'the compiler exit code' is 'compile vox' of 'the scratch path' and "vf_gen.vox" and "vf_gen_bin" and "vf_cerr".
    a text called 'the compile class' is 'classify compile exit' of 'the compiler exit code'.
    If 'the compile class' is "hang" then,
        a text called 'the hang detail' is "compiler exceeded the {'the compile budget in milliseconds'}ms compile budget (independent of --timeout, which bounds only the run)",
        a number called 'the compile hang finding' is 'finding save' of "hang" and seed and 'the source path' and 'the hang detail',
        Set stat_findings to stat_findings add 1,
        'scratch dir sweep' of 'the scratch path',
        Return a number, 1.
    If 'the compile class' is "ice" then,
        (Both a signal death (> 128) and a rust panic (101) save under the
         same "ice" category; only the detail text differs, re-derived from the
         code here so 'classify compile exit' can stay a clean category map.)
        a text called 'the ice detail' is "compiler died by signal",
        If 'the compiler exit code' is 101 then,
            Set 'the ice detail' to "compiler panicked (rust exit 101)".
        a number called 'the ice finding' is 'finding save' of "ice" and seed and 'the source path' and 'the ice detail',
        Set stat_findings to stat_findings add 1,
        'scratch dir sweep' of 'the scratch path',
        Return a number, 1.
    If 'the compile class' is "check-asm" then,
        (vox's own deliberate wrapper lines - "NASM assembly failed", "Linking
         failed" (src/main.rs:728, :911) - are the signal: they are printed on
         exactly the codegen-stage-failed path, in this exact casing, every
         time. Match those with -F first. The nasm/ld: patterns stay as
         additional alternatives for a toolchain that does echo them, but per
         FUZZER_DEFECTS.md entry 1 they were never sufficient alone: nasm's own
         diagnostic never contains "nasm", and this platform's linker prefixes
         "ld.bfd:", not "ld:". The cerr path is the per-seed one, quoted.)
        a number called 'the toolchain check' is 'run shell' of "grep -q -F -e 'NASM assembly failed' -e 'Linking failed' -e nasm -e 'ld:' {'shell quote' of 'the cerr path'}" and 5000,
        If 'the toolchain check' is 0 then,
            a number called 'the rejection finding' is 'finding save' of "asm-reject" and seed and 'the source path' and "emitted assembly refused by nasm or ld",
            Set stat_findings to stat_findings add 1,
            'scratch dir sweep' of 'the scratch path',
            Return a number, 1.
        'scratch dir sweep' of 'the scratch path',
        Return a number, 0.
    Set stat_compiled to stat_compiled add 1.
    a text called 'run1 output path' is "{'the scratch path'}/vf_out1".
    (Plan 327: the generator chose this program's argv, and BOTH runs
     must get the same one — see runner.vox. gen_argv is a global set by
     'gen build argv' during generation, so the two runs cannot drift.)
    a text called 'the verdict' is 'run program capturing with input' of 'the binary path' and 'the deadline in milliseconds' and 'run1 output path' and gen_argv and 'the input path'.
    If 'the verdict' is "hang" then,
        a number called 'the hang finding' is 'finding save' of "hang" and seed and 'the source path' and "killed at the deadline",
        Set stat_findings to stat_findings add 1,
        'scratch dir sweep' of 'the scratch path',
        Return a number, 1.
    If 'the verdict' is "signal" then,
        a number called 'the killing signal' is 'signal of' of 'the last status',
        a text called 'the detail' is "died by signal {'the killing signal'}",
        a number called 'the crash finding' is 'finding save' of "crash" and seed and 'the source path' and 'the detail',
        Set stat_findings to stat_findings add 1,
        'scratch dir sweep' of 'the scratch path',
        Return a number, 1.
    (Differential self-consistency oracle - FUZZER_DEFECTS.md defect 9.
     Only reached once the FIRST run already exited cleanly (hang/signal
     above already are findings and a second run would add nothing - the
     brief's own cost bound). A generated program takes no input and
     touches nothing but its own arithmetic and text - deterministic by
     construction - so running it again and finding different bytes on
     stdout means it printed something that MOVES, which under ASLR is
     exactly a leaked pointer (bug #39's shape). No expected value is
     needed: the output only has to disagree with itself.)
    a text called 'run2 output path' is "{'the scratch path'}/vf_out2".
    a text called 'the second verdict' is 'run program capturing with input' of 'the binary path' and 'the deadline in milliseconds' and 'run2 output path' and gen_argv and 'the input path'.
    (Plan 327 A3: an argv assertion failed. The generator chose this
     program's arguments, so it KNOWS what the flags should have parsed
     to; 91-94 mean the program disagreed. That is a wrong VALUE caught
     without a reference implementation — the one class the oracle's
     run-it-twice check cannot see, because a deterministic wrong answer
     is identical on both runs.
     These codes are reserved: 'gen program' never draws them as an
     ordinary exit code.)
    a number called 'the exit code' is 'exit code of' of 'the last status'.
    If 'the exit code' is greater than 90 and 'the exit code' is less than 95 then,
        a text called 'the assertion detail' is "argv assertion failed: exit {'the exit code'} (91 label, 92 number, 93 boolean expected true, 94 boolean expected false)",
        a number called 'the assertion finding' is 'finding save' of "wrong-value" and seed and 'the source path' and 'the assertion detail',
        Set stat_findings to stat_findings add 1,
        'scratch dir sweep' of 'the scratch path',
        Return a number, 1.
    (Exit 95: a LEDGER assertion failed - the same class of finding as
     91-94 and for the same reason (a deterministically wrong VALUE, the
     one thing the run-it-twice oracle is blind to), so it saves as
     "wrong-value" too. What differs is the detail: the generated
     program named the ledger row and both numbers on stdout just before
     exiting, and that line is worth more than any text this loop could
     write. Run 1's capture is complete - its verdict was "exit", or one
     of the branches above would already have returned.)
    If 'the exit code' is 95 then,
        a text called 'the ledger detail' is 'the assert line in file' of 'run1 output path',
        a number called 'the ledger finding' is 'finding save' of "wrong-value" and seed and 'the source path' and 'the ledger detail',
        Set stat_findings to stat_findings add 1,
        'scratch dir sweep' of 'the scratch path',
        Return a number, 1.
    If 'the second verdict' is "exit" then,
        a text called 'the first output' is 'read whole file' of 'run1 output path',
        a boolean called 'first read ok' is 'the last read succeeded',
        a text called 'the second output' is 'read whole file' of 'run2 output path',
        a boolean called 'second read ok' is 'the last read succeeded',
        If 'first read ok' then,
            If 'second read ok' then,
                If 'the first output' is not 'the second output' then,
                    a text called 'the mismatch detail' is "run 1: {'the first output'}\n---\nrun 2: {'the second output'}",
                    a number called 'the nondeterminism finding' is 'finding save' of "nondeterministic" and seed and 'the source path' and 'the mismatch detail',
                    Set stat_findings to stat_findings add 1,
                    'scratch dir sweep' of 'the scratch path',
                    Return a number, 1....
    (The four trailing periods above close, in order: the innermost
     mismatch If, 'second read ok's If, 'first read ok's If, and the
     outer exit-verdict If - periods stack, one per level (LANGUAGE.md,
     "Closing more than one level") - so a mismatched-verdict second run
     or a failed read falls straight through to here rather than being
     silently absorbed into a branch that never runs.)
    'scratch dir sweep' of 'the scratch path'.
    Return a number, 0.

(D2: a campaign whose scratch root cannot be written is total failure,
 not success - so probe the scratch root BEFORE the loop spends any time
 generating, and abort loudly if it is unusable. Provenance: a run whose
 cwd was deleted under it sailed to completion through 2,817 getcwd
 errors, compiled nothing, and still reported "findings: 0". The probe
 makes the root, writes one known byte to .vf_probe inside it, reads it
 back, compares, and deletes it - all native Vox file I/O, no shell, so a
 read-only filesystem surfaces here as a probe failure rather than as a
 shell exit code some later step could misread. Each fallible step is
 followed by 'On error Return a boolean, false.' - the first failure
 short-circuits the probe. The write step is the one that catches a
 chmod-555 scratch root: 'make directories' ignores a Create-directory
 failure on an already-present dir, but 'open for writing' inside a
 read-only directory fails with EACCES and the 'On error' fires.)
To 'scratch root is writable'.
    a number called 'the mkdir result' is 'make directories' of 'the scratch root'.
    (The probe filename carries this process's pid, for exactly the reason
     the scratch DIRECTORIES do. A fixed '.vf_probe' made this check
     reintroduce the very defect D1 exists to cure: two concurrent runs
     each created, wrote, read and DELETED the same probe file, so one
     deleted the other's mid-sequence and the loser's read or delete hit
     'On error Return a boolean, false' - reporting the scratch root as
     unwritable and aborting a campaign whose root was perfectly fine.
     Both runs lost that race as often as not, so a concurrent pair could
     abort on a false negative before generating a single program.)
    a number called 'the probe pid' is 'self pid'.
    a text called 'the probe path' is "{'the scratch root'}/.vf_probe_{'the probe pid'}".
    open a file for writing called 'the probe file' at 'the probe path'.
    On error Return a boolean, false.
    Write "x" to 'the probe file'.
    On error Return a boolean, false.
    Close 'the probe file'.
    open a file for reading called 'the probe read' at 'the probe path'.
    On error Return a boolean, false.
    a buffer called 'the probe bytes'.
    Read from 'the probe read' into 'the probe bytes'.
    On error Return a boolean, false.
    Close 'the probe read'.
    Delete the file 'the probe path'.
    On error Return a boolean, false.
    a text called 'the probe text' is 'the probe bytes' as text.
    If 'the probe text' is not "x" then,
        Return a boolean, false.
    Return a boolean, true.

To 'fuzz gen run' with a number called 'the first seed' and a number called 'how many programs' and a number called budget and a number called 'the deadline in milliseconds'.
    a boolean called 'the scratch is usable' is 'scratch root is writable'.
    If 'the scratch is usable' is false then,
        open a file for writing called 'the error stream' at 2,
        Write "vox-fuzz: scratch root '{'the scratch root'}' is not writable; aborting before the campaign.\n" to 'the error stream',
        Close 'the error stream',
        Exit 1.
    a number called 'the last seed' is 'the first seed' add 'how many programs' minus 1.
    'fuzz gen once' of each seed from 'the first seed' to 'the last seed' and budget and 'the deadline in milliseconds'.
    Print "programs: {stat_total}".
    Print "compiled: {stat_compiled}".
    Print "findings: {stat_findings}".
    (Defect 6: "could not compile anything" and "found nothing" used to
     be indistinguishable. A campaign whose working directory was
     deleted mid-flight ran to completion through 2,817 getcwd errors,
     compiled nothing, and reported a clean sweep — and a clean sweep is
     exactly what a reader wants to believe.

     The numbers were always printed side by side, so the evidence was
     there; nobody reads three lines of output as a comparison. This
     makes the comparison itself the tool's job, and makes the exit
     status carry it, because that is what a script or a CI job actually
     checks.

     Nothing compiled at all is a broken run, not a clean one: exit 2.
     A campaign that compiled less than half is not necessarily broken —
     chaos mode will do this deliberately one day — so it warns loudly
     and keeps the normal exit status rather than pretending to a
     verdict it cannot support.)
    a number called 'the failed to compile' is stat_total minus stat_compiled.
    (AND no findings. A run can legitimately compile nothing and still
     have proved something: the suite's own shim-compiler tests
     (100_asm_reject, 110_out_injection, 120_out_spaces,
     130_repro_spaced_vox) point --vox at a binary that always fails or
     panics, precisely to exercise the ICE and asm-reject paths, and
     every one of those compiles "fails" while the findings are the
     point. Firing on compiled==0 alone broke all four — the meaningless
     case is compiled nothing AND found nothing.)
    If stat_total is positive and stat_compiled is 0 and stat_findings is 0 then,
        open a file for writing called 'the compile error stream' at 2,
        Write "vox-fuzz: NOTHING COMPILED. {stat_total} programs generated, 0 compiled — this run proves nothing and 'findings: 0' above is meaningless.\n" to 'the compile error stream',
        Close 'the compile error stream',
        Exit 2.
    (UNEXPLAINED failures only. A compile that failed and produced a
     finding is accounted for — the shim-compiler tests fail every
     compile on purpose and record a finding for each, and warning about
     those would be warning about the tool working. What deserves a
     warning is a failure that produced nothing: a generated program the
     compiler simply rejected, which is a GENERATOR defect wearing a
     campaign's clothes.)
    a number called 'the unexplained' is 'the failed to compile' minus stat_findings.
    a number called 'the half' is stat_total divide 2.
    If 'the unexplained' is greater than 'the half' then,
        open a file for writing called 'the warn stream' at 2,
        Write "vox-fuzz: WARNING — {'the unexplained'} of {stat_total} programs failed to compile without producing a finding. A generated program that does not compile is a generator defect, not a finding; investigate before trusting this run.\n" to 'the warn stream',
        Close 'the warn stream'.
